      "observed": "The isolated CAN transceivers U10 and U11 have their GND1 and GND2 pins connected to the same common GND net.",
      "expected": "An isolated CAN transceiver should keep GND1 (logic side) and GND2 (CAN side) as separate ground domains so the galvanic isolation is preserved, as the specification describes isolated CAN buses.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "U10.4(GND1,power_in) U10.5(GND2,power_in) U11.4(GND1,power_in) U11.5(GND2,power_in)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "two isolated CAN buses (CAN0, CAN1), each driven by its own MCP2515 SPI CAN controller (U8, U9) through an isolated CAN transceiver (U11/companion)"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "Interface_CAN_LIN:ISO1050DUB | Isolated CAN Transceiver, SOP-8 | pins: ... 4=GND1(power_in) 5=GND2(power_in)"}
      ],
      "impact": "The CAN isolation barrier is defeated, so the buses are not isolated from the logic ground and may suffer ground loops or reduced safety/robustness.",
      "confidence": 0.85
    },
    {
      "category": "open_connection",
      "components": ["D4", "D14", "D23"],
      "pins": ["D4.2", "D14.2", "D23.2"],
      "nets": ["/LED_3.3V_POWER_SW"],
      "observed": "The net /LED_3.3V_POWER_SW connects only the anodes of LEDs D4, D14, and D23; it has no connection to +3V3 or any other supply/driver.",
      "expected": "These LED anodes should be tied to a 3.3V power net, or to a switched 3.3V supply, so the LEDs can be driven by their cathode-side signals.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "/LED_3.3V_POWER_SW : D14.2(A,passive) D23.2(A,passive) D4.2(A,passive)"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "D4 | LED_GREEN | LED_SMD:LED_0805_2012Metric | Device:LED | Light emitting diode"}
      ],
      "impact": "The Ethernet/link and PI activity LEDs connected to this net will not illuminate because their supply node is floating.",
      "confidence": 0.8
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The design files contain connectivity anomalies: the isolated CAN transceiver grounds are shorted together, defeating the specified CAN isolation, and a LED power net is left floating. These deviations would make the board behave differently from the intended isolated-CAN, status-LED design."
}

